{"book":"Judges","chapter":19,"evidence_availability":"AVAILABLE","failure_reason":null,"generated_metadata":{"commentary_prompt_version":"1.8","commentary_schema_version":"1.2","evidence_bundle_version":"1.1","evidence_hash":"b02e3808493ce199fe08888139cec54879225f955e8acb286507ef2fafd30873","generated_timestamp":null,"model":"gpt-5.6-terra","renderer_label":"commentary-v1.2-codex-session","synthesis_compiler_version":"1.1","synthesis_hash":"21423dc488b284600bec527040863dff1b3d95b287473525803ebe3737270029","synthesis_schema_version":"1.1"},"reference":"Judges 19","sections":[{"blocks":[{"confidence":"medium","evidence_ids":["judges-no-king-refrain","judges:interpretive_note:1"],"id":"block_1","interpretation_level":"disputed","synthesis_ids":["syn_interpretive_questions_b90fd1197ea5"],"text":"The opening statement that Israel had no king is part of a repeated refrain in the closing narratives. It raises the question of how the lack of a king relates to people doing what seemed right to them, but the refrain alone does not settle what form of kingship, if any, it endorses.","verse_refs":["Judges 19:1"]}],"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The Opening Refrain"},{"blocks":[{"confidence":"high","evidence_ids":["judges:interpretive_note:4"],"id":"block_2","interpretation_level":"disputed","synthesis_ids":["syn_surrounding_passages_16e80915ac6a"],"text":"The events that follow move into warfare, collective punishment, and forced wife-taking. Together with the sexual violence in this chapter, these acts are presented as signs of social collapse, not as patterns to imitate.","verse_refs":["Judges 20:1-48","Judges 21:1-25"]}],"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Larger Ending of Judges"}],"status":"validated","validation_errors":[],"validation_warnings":[]}
